3 Source Management
*******************

3.1 Listing Sources
===================

 -- Command: list [--keys]
     List all APT sources, including both active and disabled ones.
     Sources are numbered for easy reference in other commands.  If
     ‘--keys’ is specified, also shows the GPG signing key for each
     source.

     Active sources are shown normally, while disabled sources are
     marked with ‘[disabled]’ in the output.

3.2 Enabling and Disabling Sources
==================================

 -- Command: disable ID
     Disable a source by renaming it with a ‘.disabled’ extension.  The
     source remains on the system but is not used by APT. This is safer
     than deleting sources as they can be easily re-enabled.

     Cannot be used on the main ‘/etc/apt/sources.list’ file.

 -- Command: enable ID
     Enable a previously disabled source by removing the ‘.disabled’
     extension.  The source becomes active immediately but requires ‘apt
     update’ to refresh package lists.

 -- Command: list-disabled
     Show only disabled sources.  This is useful for reviewing what
     sources are available but not currently active.

3.3 Package Operations
======================

 -- Command: show ID
     Show all packages available in the source identified by ID.  This
     queries the repository's package index directly.

 -- Command: installed ID
     Show all packages currently installed on your system that came from
     the source identified by ID.

 -- Command: remove ID
     Remove all packages installed from the source identified by ID.
     This is useful before removing a third-party repository to avoid
     leaving orphaned packages.

     *Warning:* This operation removes packages without confirmation.
     Use with caution.

3.4 Security Audit
==================

 -- Command: lint
     Run comprehensive security audit of all sources and keys.  Performs
     17 different checks including:
        • Deprecated keyring files
        • Legacy .list file formats
        • Sources without explicit keys
        • Missing or unreadable key files
        • Keys in legacy locations
        • Expired or expiring keys
        • HTTP sources (should use HTTPS)
        • Weak key algorithms
        • Duplicate sources
        • Unreachable URLs
        • End-of-Life releases
        • File permissions and ownership
        • Missing security repositories
        • Development/testing repositories
        • Unnecessary deb-src entries
        • Architecture mismatches

 -- Command: lint --fix
     Interactively fix security warnings found by the audit.  This
     command provides a safe, interactive way to apply fixes:

        • Scans for HTTP sources and tests if HTTPS is available before
          converting
        • Moves legacy keys from ‘trusted.gpg.d/’ to
          ‘/etc/apt/keyrings/’
        • Automatically updates .sources files with new key paths
        • Removes old keys after successful copy (backups saved)
        • Detects unreachable sources and offers to disable or remove
          them
        • Prompts for confirmation before each change
        • Creates timestamped backup directory for all modified files
        • Tests URL reachability before making changes
        • Provides clear instructions for any remaining manual steps
        • Shows summary of applied, skipped, and failed fixes

     *Unreachable Sources:* When an unreachable source is detected, you
     can choose to:
        • *d)* Disable the source (rename to .disabled) - safe,
          reversible
        • *r)* Remove the source file completely - permanent deletion
          with backup
        • *s)* Skip and leave as is

     *Safety Features:*
        • All changes require explicit confirmation
        • Backups are created before any modifications
        • HTTPS URLs are tested before conversion
        • Instructions provided for complex operations
        • No changes to main ‘sources.list’
        • Removal requires double confirmation

     Example:
          sudo apt-man lint --fix

3.5 Security Fixes
==================

 -- Command: migrate ID
     Convert a legacy .list file to modern .sources (DEB822) format.
     Currently provides manual migration steps.  The modern format is
     more structured and supports explicit key references via the
     Signed-By field.

     *Note:* This feature currently guides you through manual migration
     steps.  Automatic migration is planned for a future release.

 -- Command: use-https ID
     Convert an HTTP source to HTTPS for improved security.  The
     command:
        • Tests URL reachability before making changes
        • Creates a backup of the original file
        • Updates all HTTP URLs in the file to HTTPS
        • Provides instructions for testing with ‘apt update’

     *Note:* Some repositories may not support HTTPS. The command will
     fail gracefully if the HTTPS URL is not reachable.

3.6 Upgrading Sources
=====================

 -- Command: upgrade-source OLD NEW
     Upgrade all APT sources from OLD release codename to NEW release
     codename.  This modifies all source files to reference the new
     release.

     For example, to upgrade from Ubuntu Noble to Oracular:
          apt-man upgrade-source noble oracular

     After upgrading sources, run:
          sudo apt update
          sudo apt dist-upgrade


