4 Key Management
****************

4.1 Listing Keys
================

 -- Command: keys [--list]
     List all GPG keys on the system used for package verification.
     Keys are categorized by their format and location, helping identify
     outdated key storage methods.  This is the default action when
     using ‘keys’ without any options.

4.2 Checking Keys
=================

 -- Command: keys --check
     Validate all sources for missing or problematic GPG keys.  This
     command checks:

        • Whether referenced key files exist
        • Whether key files are readable
        • Sources without explicit keys (potential security risk)
        • Key format (old vs new)

     Reports any issues found with actionable information.

4.3 Refreshing Keys
===================

 -- Command: keys --refresh
     Refresh GPG keys from keyservers to get updates, revocations, or
     new signatures.  This only affects keys in ‘/etc/apt/keyrings/’.

     Keys in ‘/usr/share/keyrings/’ are managed by APT packages and
     should be updated via ‘apt upgrade’.

4.4 Key Information
===================

 -- Command: keys --info KEYFILE
     Show detailed information about a specific GPG key file, including:

        • Key ID
        • Algorithm and key strength
        • Creation date
        • Expiration date (if any)
        • Whether the key is expired
        • Key UIDs (identities)

     Example:
          apt-man keys --info /etc/apt/keyrings/microsoft.gpg

4.5 Moving Legacy Keys
======================

 -- Command: keys --move KEYFILE
     Move a legacy key from ‘/etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/’ to the modern
     location ‘/etc/apt/keyrings/’.  This command:

        • Creates a timestamped backup in ‘/tmp/’
        • Validates the key is in the legacy location
        • Creates ‘/etc/apt/keyrings/’ if it doesn't exist
        • Copies the key to the new location
        • Sets appropriate file permissions
        • Automatically updates all ‘.sources’ files that reference the
          old key path
        • Removes the key from the legacy location

     *Automatic Updates:* The command scans all ‘.sources’ files in
     ‘/etc/apt/sources.list.d/’ and automatically replaces references to
     the old key path with the new path.  Each updated file is backed up
     before modification.

     *Testing:* After the key is moved, test with ‘apt update’ to ensure
     everything works.  If there are problems, restore the backups from
     ‘/tmp/’.

     Example:
          apt-man keys --move /etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/old.gpg

4.6 Key Renewal Planning
========================

 -- Command: keys --renewal
     Show all GPG keys that will expire in the next 90 days.  This helps
     plan for key renewals before they cause APT update failures.  For
     each expiring key, displays:

        • Key file path
        • Key ID
        • Expiration date
        • Days remaining until expiration

     Regular use of this command helps maintain system security and
     avoid surprise APT failures due to expired keys.


