5 Understanding Key Formats
***************************

5.1 Legacy Formats (Deprecated)
===============================

5.1.1 OLD (deprecated)
----------------------

The single-file ‘/etc/apt/trusted.gpg’ keyring.  This format is
deprecated and should not be used for new keys.  All keys in this file
are automatically trusted by APT.

5.1.2 OLD (trusted.gpg.d)
-------------------------

Keys stored in ‘/etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/*.gpg’.  While better than the
single-file approach, this is still an older format.  Keys here are
automatically trusted by APT without explicit source references.

5.2 Modern Formats (Recommended)
================================

5.2.1 NEW (apt keyring)
-----------------------

Keys in ‘/etc/apt/keyrings/*.gpg’.  This is the recommended location for
user-managed keys.  Sources must explicitly reference these keys using
the ‘Signed-By’ field in DEB822 format or equivalent options.

   This approach provides better security through explicit trust
relationships.

5.2.2 NEW (system keyring)
--------------------------

Keys in ‘/usr/share/keyrings/*.gpg’.  These are installed and updated
via APT packages (e.g., ‘ubuntu-keyring’).  Generally used for
distribution-provided keys.

5.3 Special Formats
===================

5.3.1 INLINE (embedded key)
---------------------------

PGP keys embedded directly in DEB822 ‘.sources’ files using the
‘Signed-By’ field.  These cannot be refreshed from keyservers but are
self-contained within the source definition.


