8 Security Considerations
*************************

8.1 Key Verification
====================

Always verify GPG keys before adding them to your system:

  1. Download the key from the official source
  2. Verify the key fingerprint matches published values
  3. Check the key's UIDs and signatures
  4. Store in ‘/etc/apt/keyrings/’ with appropriate permissions

8.2 Best Practices
==================

   • Use explicit ‘Signed-By’ references in ‘.sources’ files
   • Prefer keys in ‘/etc/apt/keyrings/’ over ‘/etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/’
   • Regularly run ‘apt-man --check-keys’ to identify issues
   • Keep keys updated, especially for third-party repositories
   • Remove unused sources and their keys
   • Be cautious with sources that don't specify keys
   • Review key expiration dates periodically

8.3 Common Security Issues
==========================

8.3.1 Missing Keys
------------------

Sources without keys or with missing key files can be compromised.
Always ensure keys are present and valid.

8.3.2 Expired Keys
------------------

Expired keys may indicate abandoned repositories or security issues.
Check with ‘apt-man --key-info’.

8.3.3 Legacy Key Formats
------------------------

Keys in ‘/etc/apt/trusted.gpg’ or ‘/etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/’ are
automatically trusted.  Migrate to explicit references when possible.


